## Strategy
You are doing great! Sorting an array of structs is actually very similar to the Bubble Sort logic we saw in `demo_problem_sort.c`. The secret is that instead of comparing simple integers like `arr[j] > arr[j + 1]`, you simply access the specific field inside your struct using the dot operator (`.`). If you have an array of students and you want to sort by their `grade`, you just compare `students[j].grade > students[j + 1].grade`. Everything else—the nested loops and the swapping logic—remains exactly the same!

## Visual Logic
```mermaid
graph TD
    A["Start Sorting"] --> B["Compare adjacent structs"]
    B -->|Is current.field > next.field?| C["Swap the entire struct"]
    C --> D["Move to next pair"]
    B -->|No| D
    D --> E["End of array?"]
    E -->|No| B
    E -->|Yes| F["Sorted!"]
```

## Implementation Plan
1. **Access the Field**: Use the dot operator to reach the specific member you want to compare.
2. **Swap the Structs**: When you find an out-of-order pair, use a temporary variable of the same struct type to hold the data while you swap the entire struct at once.

```c
// Assuming you have: struct Student { int grade; char name[20]; };
// And an array: struct Student class[5];

// Inside your nested loops:
if (class[j].grade > class[j + 1].grade) {
    // Create a temporary struct to hold the data during the swap
    struct Student temp = class[j];
    class[j] = class[j + 1];
    class[j + 1] = temp;
}
```

